//! member: true
//!
//! std.set — `Set[T]`, the unordered membership collection. Stability
//! tier: core (the API; the implementation is blocked).
//!
//! **This module has no code yet, and that is the honest state.** A
//! `Set[T]` is a std-DEFINED generic data type — no implementation
//! provides a set builtin, and `struct Set[T] { … }` does not parse in
//! either implementation at the pins: a generic parameter list on a data
//! declaration is E0201 in both (s16 delivers generic data types; the
//! observation is filed with F-0011). Writing a concrete `SetInt` here
//! instead would ship the wrong API — callers would migrate twice — so
//! the inventory below is a reviewed CONTRACT, not a stub to be filled
//! in by guesswork, and it lands as wolf source in the sprint that
//! follows s16.
//!
//! Reviewed inventory (13), each with its contract, exactly as it will
//! land. `Eq` is the membership protocol; `Hash` is F-0011's open
//! question, same as `Map`'s keys.
//!
//! ```text
//! add[T: Eq](mut s: Set[T], v: T) -> bool
//!     Inserts `v`; true when the set grew, false when `v` was present.
//!     Total: no error row, no traps beyond allocation.
//! remove[T: Eq](mut s: Set[T], v: T) -> bool
//!     Removes `v`; true when it was there. No row: absence is the
//!     answer, not an error.
//! has[T: Eq](s: Set[T], v: T) -> bool
//!     Membership. Total.
//! len[T](s: Set[T]) -> int
//!     Number of distinct elements. Total.
//! is_empty[T](s: Set[T]) -> bool
//!     `len(s) == 0`. Total.
//! clear[T](mut s: Set[T])
//!     Empties `s`, keeping it usable. Total.
//! to_list[T](s: Set[T]) -> List[T]
//!     Fresh list of the elements; order UNSPECIFIED (documented, not
//!     promised). Allocates (`to_`, §1).
//! union[T: Eq](a: Set[T], b: Set[T]) -> Set[T]
//!     Elements in either; a fresh set, both operands read.
//! intersect[T: Eq](a: Set[T], b: Set[T]) -> Set[T]
//!     Elements in both.
//! difference[T: Eq](a: Set[T], b: Set[T]) -> Set[T]
//!     Elements of `a` not in `b` (asymmetric — the name says so).
//! symmetric_difference[T: Eq](a: Set[T], b: Set[T]) -> Set[T]
//!     Elements in exactly one of the two.
//! is_subset[T: Eq](a: Set[T], b: Set[T]) -> bool
//!     Every element of `a` is in `b`; the empty set is a subset of
//!     everything, and a set is a subset of itself.
//! is_disjoint[T: Eq](a: Set[T], b: Set[T]) -> bool
//!     No shared element; two empty sets are disjoint.
//! ```
//!
//! No tests ship for this module: there is nothing to run, and a test
//! that imports an itemless module is E0305 (unused import) in both
//! implementations. The ledger stays honest by having no row to lie in.
